  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Further Venn Diagrams"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "What do you mean by further Venn diagrams?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The Venn diagrams used here are no more complicated than those in the first **Venn Diagrams **revision note, however\n\n  - questions may use **set** **notation** as well, or alongside contextual questions\n  - there may be more detailed use of **conditional probability**\n  - **mutually** **exclusive**, three events and other unusual Venn diagram setups may be involved"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/05/2-3-3-cie-fig0-special-venn.png",
   "file": "assets/e0767174ffc4-2-3-3-cie-fig0-special-venn.png",
   "alt": "2-3-3-cie-fig0-special-venn",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "How do I solve conditional probability problems using Venn diagrams?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Interpreting questions in terms of **AND** ($\\cup$), **OR** ($\\cap$),** complement** ( ‘ ) and\n**“given that”** ( | )\n- Use mini-Venn diagrams to sketch and shade the regions you are dealing with – use different colours if available or different styles of shading if not\n\n  - Shading can help you see the answer"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "since $\\mathrm{P} ( A \\vert B ) = \\frac{ \\mathrm{P} ( A \\cap B )}{ \\mathrm{P} ( B )}$ shade *B*  first, then shade $A\\capB$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "the answer will then be $\\frac{\"\\mathrm{double} \\mathrm{shading}\"}{\"\\mathrm{single} \\mathrm{shading}\"}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/05/plx9zFR~_3-2-2-fig1-set-notation-examples.png",
   "file": "assets/d6748c00ddb8-plx9zfr-3-2-2-fig1-set-notation-examples.png",
   "alt": "plx9zFR~_3-2-2-fig1-set-notation-examples",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Three events, $A,B$ and $C$ are such that\n\nevents $A$ and $C$ are mutually exclusive\n\n$P(A\\capB)=0.2\\,P(B\\capC)=0.3\\,P((A\\cupB\\cupC)')=0.1\\,P(B)=0.7\\,P(A')=0.75.$\n\nFind\n\n(i) $\\mathrm{P} ( A \\vert B )$\n\n(ii) $\\mathrm{P} ( A ' \\vert C ' )$\n\n(iii) $\\mathrm{P} ( C \\vert ( A \\cup B ) ' )$\n\n**Answer:**\n\n![2-3-3-ciie-fig2-we-solution_part1](assets/2c29ba6e9181-image.bin)\n\n![2-3-3-ciie-fig2-we-solution_part2](assets/73ec553c02ba-image.bin)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Although very versatile, Venn diagrams may not always be the best option\n\n  - use a tree diagram if one event follows another\n\ne.g.  two beads drawn from a bag without replacement\n\n- use a two-way table for showing how many items/people are in associated categories\ne.g.  A school year group split by gender selecting which sport they wish to play"
  }
 ]
}